  "response": "The culprit is `sync_io_in_hotpath` in `loadPlanSchema`. Its evidence is:\n\n> \"`existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)\"  \n> \"`statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)\"  \n> \"`readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)\"  \n> \"lines: 12, 13, 15\"  \n> \"swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it\"\n\nThe blocking methods are `existsSync`, `statSync`, and `readFileSync`.\n\nThe smallest migration is to use `node:fs/promises`:\n\n```ts\nimport { readFile, stat } from \"node:fs/promises\";\n\nexport async function loadPlanSchema(/* existing parameters */) {\n  try {\n    const fileStat = await stat(schemaPath);\n    if (!fileStat.isFile()) return /* existing missing-file result */;\n\n    const contents = await readFile(schemaPath, \"utf8\");\n    return /* existing parsing/result logic */;\n  } catch (error) {\n    if (\n      error instanceof Error &&\n      \"code\" in error &&\n      error.code === \"ENOENT\"\n    ) {\n      return /* existing missing-file result */;\n    }\n    throw error;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThere is no need for an async `exists` replacement: attempting `stat()` and handling `ENOENT` avoids the check-then-use race and reduces three filesystem operations to two. If `loadPlanSchema` already returns a `Promise`, its public signature stays unchanged; otherwise preserve the production handler’s signature by making this helper async and awaiting it inside the already-async handler. A genuinely asynchronous migration cannot preserve a synchronous return type—the async boundary must surface at least as far as an existing Promise-returning caller.",
